                    briefing_text_r2:       `Set the sourcing architecture per product family and size the buffer. Keep cheap lean ocean for basics, add a near-shore agile source for performance SKUs, and place a <strong>decoupling point</strong> between them — that is leagile. Every dollar of buffer eats the line: inventory is US$96M against US$110M, so more than <strong>US$14M</strong> of new safety stock breaches the covenant. Blanket buffering lifts fill rate fast but traps cash on basics whose demand you can forecast in your sleep. Buffer <em>where demand is variable</em>, not everywhere.`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Respond to the shock & invest in alertness`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The live decisions. Expedite the viral SKU by air at <strong>US$4.20/unit</strong> versus US$0.45 ocean — on a tent netting US$18 margin it pays, but flying the basics in the same lane burns cash at near-zero margin, so apply a <strong>SKU-level margin test</strong>. Decide the US$22M of slow basics: clear them now, or hold and stay long the wrong inventory while you ration the right one. And invest in <strong>alertness</strong> — control-tower visibility and demand-sensing. Alertness and agility are <em>distinct</em>: speed you cannot aim is wasted, and seeing the shock without the capacity to respond is just as useless.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Commit the resilience configuration`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Brief the board and the bank. Show a coherent configuration: which products run <strong>lean</strong>, which run <strong>agile</strong>, where the decoupling point sits, and which investments are standing <strong>capability</strong> (alertness, dual-source optionality) versus one-off patches (this quarter's air freight, the markdown). Prove the design lifts fill rate and the agility index <em>without</em> breaching the working-capital line — and that it is robust to the <em>next</em> shock, a supplier fire or a demand collapse, not just this port strike.`,

