                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_segment:       `<strong>That is the diagnosis.</strong> Basics are functional and predictable — they reward a lean chain; performance SKUs are innovative and volatile — they reward an agile one. Running one design for both is the original sin behind your simultaneous stockout and markdown. Now build two chains, not one.`,
                    coach_r1_leanall:       `<strong>'It is all one catalog' is exactly the mistake that put you here.</strong> One lean chain optimised for cost cannot serve a viral tent, and it traps cash in basics you cannot move. The stockout and the markdown are the same design failure — segment by demand type or repeat it next season.`,
                    coach_r1_agileall:      `<strong>Making the whole network fast is the opposite mistake.</strong> Your basics — stable, price-sensitive, 60% of volume — just got more expensive to make, and that is your thin-margin line. Why pay for responsiveness on demand you can forecast in your sleep?`,
                    coach_r1_volume:        `<strong>Volume is the wrong axis.</strong> A high-volume SKU can still be volatile, and a low-volume one perfectly predictable. Lean-vs-agile is decided by demand <em>variability</em>, not by how many units you ship. Re-segment on the demand profile.`,
                    coach_r1_sharp:         `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You segmented by demand type AND named alertness as the worst dimension — you learned about the disruption three weeks late. That is the lens that lets the buffer, sourcing and visibility decisions reinforce each other instead of fighting.`,
                    coach_r1_preparedness:  `<strong>'More buffer everywhere' is the reflex, not the answer.</strong> Preparedness matters, but your real gap is alertness — you found out about the port strike from a customer. More safety stock on predictable basics is trapped cash, not resilience.`,
                    coach_r1_fine:          `<strong>'A bad-luck quarter' is what you tell the board right before the next one.</strong> A port strike, a viral SKU — these are the disruptions a resilient design expects. If you believe the configuration is fine, you will rebuild exactly the chain that failed.`,
                    coach_r1_lowfocus:      `<strong>If the demand-supply fit map is not the spine of the plan, every later decision drifts.</strong> Without a clear lean/agile split driving the buffer and sourcing choices, you will end up blanket-buffering and one-size sourcing all over again.`,
                    coach_r2_leagile:       `<strong>Leagile is the configured answer.</strong> Lean ocean for the predictable basics, a near-shore agile source for the volatile performance SKUs, and a decoupling point between them — you serve the hot SKU without making the thin-margin basics more expensive. Now position the buffer to match.`,
                    coach_r2_lean:          `<strong>Riding out the lead-time hit on one lean chain leaves the viral SKU starved.</strong> 115-day ocean cannot catch an 8-week demand window. You will keep the cheap unit cost and keep losing the full-margin sale — the cheapest source is the most expensive supply chain here.`,
                    coach_r2_agile:         `<strong>All near-shore makes the whole portfolio responsive — and 14% more expensive to make.</strong> On basics that are 60% of volume and your thinnest margin, that premium is pure waste. You bought agility where demand is perfectly forecastable. Decouple it.`,
                    coach_r2_selective:     `<strong>Selective buffering on the variable line is the disciplined move.</strong> Safety stock on the performance SKUs and the decoupling point lifts fill where demand is hard to forecast, while basics stay lean. That is preparedness matched to variability, not blanket cash-trapping.`,
                    coach_r2_blanket:       `<strong>Fill rate's back up — and you just pushed inventory toward the line.</strong> Blanket safety stock buys preparedness for predictable basics that never needed it. The bank's covenant call is the price. Where specifically does buffering pay, and where is it just trapped cash?`,
                    coach_r2_basics:        `<strong>Buffering the basics first protects the one line you can already forecast.</strong> Their demand is stable — that safety stock sits as trapped cash while the viral SKU stays short. Move the buffer to where the variability actually is.`,
                    coach_r3_test:          `<strong>A margin test is exactly the discipline.</strong> Flying only the hot units whose recovered margin beats the US$4.20 premium recovers full-margin demand without burning cash on basics. Expediting is a scalpel, not a firehose — and you held the scalpel.`,
                    coach_r3_alltest:       `<strong>Flying the whole container 'to sort margins out later' has no margin test.</strong> Even at a modest air share you are paying the 8–10x premium on basics that net almost nothing. What was the SKU-level test?`,
                    coach_r3_firehose:      `<strong>You airfreighted the whole replenishment at US$4.20/unit versus US$0.45 ocean.</strong> Fine on a tent netting US$18 — but you flew the basics in the same lane at near-zero incremental margin. Expediting is a scalpel, not a firehose. What was the margin test?`,
                    coach_r3_nofly:         `<strong>Flying nothing concedes the full-margin demand.</strong> The viral window closes in weeks and 115-day ocean will not make it. There was ~US$14M of full-margin demand to recover — the freight premium on the hot SKU alone was easily justified.`,
                    coach_r3_hold:          `<strong>Holding the slow basics 'hoping demand returns' while you ration the hot SKU is one failure, not two.</strong> You are long the wrong inventory and short the right one — simultaneously. Clear the basics and free the cash for where demand actually is.`,
                    coach_r3_linked:        `<strong>You connected the two symptoms.</strong> Clearing the slow basics frees cash and headroom while you expedite the hot SKU with a margin test — the long and the short side of the same mismatch, fixed by one coherent move.`,
                    coach_r3_noalert:       `<strong>You built a fast response capability and stayed blind.</strong> You will hear about the next disruption from a retailer's complaint, three weeks late — speed you cannot aim is wasted. How much sooner would a control-tower signal have let you react, and what is that worth versus the response capacity you bought?`,
                    coach_r4_coherent:      `<strong>That is a coherent configuration.</strong> Lean basics, agile performance, a decoupling point, selective buffers and alertness as standing capability — and you proved it inside the working-capital line. This is resilience as a design, not a reflex.`,
                    coach_r4_overbuilt:     `<strong>Maximum resilience everywhere breaches the very line the board is watching.</strong> Agile sourcing and heavy buffers across predictable basics is exactly the blanket move that triggers the covenant. Resilience you cannot finance is not resilience.`,
                    coach_r4_status:        `<strong>Going back to the one lean chain rebuilds the failure.</strong> It was cheapest on unit cost and most expensive on total cost — the stockout, the markdown and the covenant scare all came from that single design. 'A freak quarter' is what precedes the next freak quarter.`,
                    coach_r4_patch:         `<strong>A coherent design, but you called it all one-off.</strong> If none of it is standing capability, you will rebuild from scratch when the next shock hits. Name the durable parts — alertness and dual-source optionality — or you are firefighting forever.`,
                    coach_r4_thisonly:      `<strong>A plan tuned only to this port strike solves the shock you already survived.</strong> The next one is a supplier fire, a tariff or a demand collapse. Which of your investments still helps then? Resilience is being ready for the shock you cannot name yet.`,
                    coach_r4_robust:        `<strong>That is durable resilience.</strong> Coherent leagile design, alertness as standing capability, and dual-source optionality that helps against a shock you cannot name yet — not a patch for April. The board has a configuration; the bank has its line intact.`,
                    coach_breach:           `<strong>You breached the working-capital line.</strong> Inventory above US$110M is exactly the covenant trigger the bank flagged. Whatever fill rate you bought, you bought it with cash you do not have — pull the buffer back and target it.`,

